<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tylos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tylos was the Greek name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> in the Hellenistic and Roman periods, first recorded by writers drawing on the voyages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s officers. The island was known for its pearls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and belonged to trade networks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> and later of Characene and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>. Archaeologists use the name for the Tylos period of Bahraini history, from about 300 BCE to 600 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tylos.jpg" alt="Tylos" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Walker, John · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapofqatar1849.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Tylos is thought to be a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenization">Hellenisation</a> of the Semitic Tilmun, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a>. In the classical era the island was a centre of maritime trade and pearling in the Erythraean Sea, and from the 6th to the 3rd century BCE it was part of the Persian Empire. After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, his admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nearchus">Nearchus</a> led an expedition that reached the island, and Androsthenes of Thasos, who served under him, left an extensive account of it in the Periplus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Later writers drew on it, among them the botanist Theophrastus, who described the island as a rich source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>.</p><p>The Greek geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> named islands in the Persian Gulf Tyre and Arad, the latter now Muharraq, and recorded a local legend that they were the mother cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> reports a Tyrian tradition that the Phoenicians came from the Persian Gulf. The paired names have been much discussed as possible signs of colonisation in one direction or the other, but there is little evidence of any occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> at the time such a migration would have taken place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> is not known, though the site of Qalat Al Bahrain has been proposed as a Seleucid base. Tylos was part of the Hellenised world: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> was in everyday use, while the upper classes spoke Greek. Local coins show a seated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, who may have been worshipped as a form of the Arabian sun-god Shams, and the island held Greek athletic contests. As Seleucid power waned it passed to Mesene, the kingdom founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyspaosines">Hyspaosines</a> in 129 BCE, which ruled it until the 2nd century CE, through a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> and later, under King Meredates, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>.</p><p>In the 3rd century CE the Sassanids succeeded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> as overlords of Mesene, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> was later incorporated into the Sassanid province of Mazon on the southern shore of the Gulf. By the 5th century it was a centre of Nestorian Christianity with an episcopal see at Samahij, and in 410, according to the synodal records of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> church, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> named Batai was excommunicated there. A shark deity called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain-island">Awal</a> was also worshipped, and for many centuries afterwards the islands were known as Awal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tylos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The island remained a trading centre after Dilmun disappeared, known to Greek authors as <b>Tylos</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
